{{!-- =====================================================
     RAPID FIRE METHODOLOGY STORYBOARDING
===================================================== --}}

<main class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">

    {{!-- =================================================
         INTRODUCTION
    ================================================== --}}

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        METHODOLOGY
      </p>

      <h1>
        Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Build the work plan before you write it. Rapid Fire Methodology
        Storyboarding helps the proposal team turn the RFP scope into a
        logical, coordinated plan for delivering the project.
      </p>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         QUICK FACTS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-quick-facts"
    >

      <h2 id="methodology-quick-facts">
        At a Glance
      </h2>

      <div class="sasha-practice-fact-grid">

        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            PURPOSE
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Plan before writing
          </p>

          <p>
            Bring the proposal team together to develop the logic,
            activities, responsibilities, deliverables, and dependencies
            before anyone starts drafting methodology prose.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            BEST FOR
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            Full Effort Pursuits
          </p>

          <p>
            Particularly valuable for competitive, multidisciplinary,
            technically complex pursuits where the methodology carries
            significant evaluation weight.
          </p>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-fact-card">

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-label">
            OUTPUT
          </p>

          <p class="sasha-practice-fact-value">
            A proposal-ready work plan
          </p>

          <p>
            The session produces a structured methodology that Sasha can
            use as the foundation for writing the proposal section.
          </p>

        </article>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHY STORYBOARD FIRST
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-why"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHY IT MATTERS
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-why">
            The difficult work should happen before the writing.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            A methodology should not be a generic list of engineering
            activities or a rewritten version of the RFP scope.
          </p>

          <p>
            It should show the evaluator that the proposed team has
            thought carefully about how this particular project will
            actually be delivered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding gets that thinking out
            of people's heads and into one coordinated work plan before
            the proposal writer begins turning it into prose.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHO PARTICIPATES
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-participants"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHO PARTICIPATES
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-participants">
            Bring the people who will actually deliver the work.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          The objective is to capture the knowledge of the proposed team,
          not leave the methodology to one proposal writer working alone.
          Include the people who understand the scope, technical issues,
          interfaces, risks, schedule, and client.
        </p>


        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Proposed Project Manager
            </strong>

            <span>
              Leads the delivery thinking and helps establish how the
              project should progress from one stage to the next.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Technical Leads
            </strong>

            <span>
              Define the activities, technical decisions, risks,
              interfaces, inputs, and deliverables within their areas.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Discipline Leads
            </strong>

            <span>
              Identify where disciplines depend on one another and where
              coordination must occur.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Proposal Manager
            </strong>

            <span>
              Facilitates the session, checks the RFP scope, records the
              storyboard, and keeps the discussion moving.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Client-Facing Staff
            </strong>

            <span>
              Contribute knowledge about client expectations, working
              preferences, concerns, and important project context.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Specialists as Needed
            </strong>

            <span>
              Bring in environmental, stakeholder, construction,
              approvals, risk, operations, or other specialists where
              their work materially affects the methodology.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         START WITH THE RFP
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-start"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            START HERE
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-start">
            Begin with the RFP scope.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          Before the team invents its own structure, identify everything
          the client has asked the successful consultant to do.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Scope items</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Tasks and phases</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Deliverables</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Milestones</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Client responsibilities</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Approvals and decisions</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Meetings</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Schedule requirements</span>
          </div>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Account for the entire requested scope.
          </strong>

          <span>
            If something is missing, ambiguous, contradictory, or poorly
            defined, flag it. Do not quietly invent the missing work.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         BUILD THE WORK BREAKDOWN STRUCTURE
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-process"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            BUILD THE STORYBOARD
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-process">
            Work through each task systematically.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Scope Item
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify the requirement in the RFP that this task addresses.
              Every required scope item needs a clear home in the work plan.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Key Issues
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify the technical, operational, regulatory, stakeholder,
              environmental, schedule, financial, or project-management
              issues affecting this task.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Ask why this task could be difficult.
              </strong>

              <span>
                The answer helps turn a generic work plan into one designed
                for this project.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Objective
            </h3>

            <p>
              Define what the task must accomplish. An objective describes
              the result the team is trying to achieve, not the work it
              performs to get there.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Activities
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify what the team will actually do. Be specific enough
              that the evaluator can understand how the objective will be
              achieved.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Value-Added Elements
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify improvements to the basic approach that reduce risk,
              improve coordination, accelerate decisions, prevent rework,
              improve information, or otherwise create meaningful value.
            </p>

            <p class="sasha-practice-step-note">
              Value added does not simply mean doing more work.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Deliverables
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify what exists when the task is complete: a decision,
              design, recommendation, model, approval, report, drawing,
              memorandum, or other tangible output.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Personnel
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify who needs to participate and why. Include the
              disciplines and specialists whose involvement materially
              affects the work.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>


        <article class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Timing and Dependencies
            </h3>

            <p>
              Determine when the task occurs, what information it needs,
              what it produces for later tasks, and what must happen before
              the next activity can begin.
            </p>

          </div>

        </article>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         THREE IMPORTANT DISTINCTIONS
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-distinctions"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
          KEEP THESE STRAIGHT
        </p>

        <h2 id="methodology-distinctions">
          Objective, activity, and deliverable are different things.
        </h2>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Objective
            </strong>

            <span>
              What does this task need to accomplish?
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Activity
            </strong>

            <span>
              What will the team actually do?
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Deliverable
            </strong>

            <span>
              What tangible result exists when the activity is complete?
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         CONNECT THE WORK
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-connections"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            SHOW THE FLOW
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-connections">
            Projects are not collections of independent tasks.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Show where one activity produces information needed by another,
            where decisions control downstream work, and where approvals
            or client input affect the sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make multidisciplinary interfaces visible. The client is hiring
            one project team, not a collection of isolated specialists.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the relationships become difficult to explain in prose,
            consider a workflow, schedule, process diagram, or other graphic.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         CLIENT + RISK + STRATEGY
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-strengthen"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            STRENGTHEN THE PLAN
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-strengthen">
            Make the methodology specific to this pursuit.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span            <span>
              Client priorities and concerns
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Known project risks
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Win strategy and win themes
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Competitive differentiators
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Lessons from similar projects
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Team strengths and relevant experience
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Do not bolt strategy onto the methodology afterward.
          </strong>

          <span>
            If the pursuit strategy says that schedule certainty,
            stakeholder coordination, constructability, risk reduction,
            or another issue matters to the client, the work plan should
            show how the team will actually deliver that advantage.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         RAPID FIRE SESSION
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-session"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            RUN THE SESSION
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-session">
            Keep it rapid, practical, and collaborative.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p>
          Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding is not intended to become
          a long technical workshop. The purpose is to draw out the team's
          knowledge quickly enough to build the backbone of the methodology.
        </p>


        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Work one task at a time
            </strong>

            <span>
              Keep the discussion focused on the current scope item before
              moving to the next part of the work.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Ask short questions
            </strong>

            <span>
              What is difficult? What are we trying to accomplish? What do
              we do? Who does it? What does it produce? What happens next?
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Capture first, polish later
            </strong>

            <span>
              Record the team's thinking in concise phrases. Do not stop
              the session to perfect proposal language.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Challenge generic answers
            </strong>

            <span>
              If the same answer could appear in almost any proposal,
              ask what is different about this project, client, site,
              team, or delivery environment.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Resolve contradictions
            </strong>

            <span>
              When team members describe different approaches, identify
              the disagreement and establish the approach the proposal
              will actually present.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Keep moving
            </strong>

            <span>
              Flag questions that require research or follow-up rather
              than allowing one unresolved issue to consume the session.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         QUESTIONS TO ASK
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-questions"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            RAPID FIRE QUESTIONS
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-questions">
            Questions that draw out the real work plan.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            What exactly has the client asked us to accomplish?
          </p>

          <p>
            What makes this task difficult on this particular project?
          </p>

          <p>
            What do we need to know before we can begin?
          </p>

          <p>
            What will we actually do?
          </p>

          <p>
            Who needs to be involved?
          </p>

          <p>
            Where do disciplines need to coordinate?
          </p>

          <p>
            What decisions or approvals are required?
          </p>

          <p>
            What can go wrong, and how will our approach reduce that risk?
          </p>

          <p>
            What will the client receive when this task is complete?
          </p>

          <p>
            What does the next task need from this one?
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         WHAT GOOD LOOKS LIKE
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-good"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WHAT GOOD LOOKS LIKE
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-good">
            A strong storyboard should make the project feel manageable.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Every required scope item is accounted for
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Objectives are clear
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Activities explain how the work will be performed
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Deliverables are identifiable
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Responsibilities are visible
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Dependencies and interfaces are understood
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Project-specific risks influence the approach
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Client priorities are reflected in how the work is delivered
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Value-added ideas solve real project problems
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              The complete work plan tells one coordinated story
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         FROM STORYBOARD TO PROPOSAL
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-proposal"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            FROM PLAN TO PROPOSAL
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-proposal">
            Write from the storyboard — not from a blank page.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Once the storyboard is complete, the proposal writer has the
            substance needed to develop the methodology section.
          </p>

          <p>
            The writing should preserve the logic created by the team:
            what needs to be accomplished, what the team will do, who will
            do it, what the work produces, and how each activity connects
            to the next.
          </p>

          <p>
            The storyboard can also reveal where a schedule, workflow,
            responsibility matrix, process diagram, risk table, or other
            graphic will explain the approach more effectively than prose.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         COMMON FAILURE MODES
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-failures"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <div>

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WATCH FOR THESE
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-failures">
            Common methodology mistakes.
          </h2>

        </div>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Repeating the RFP
            </strong>

            <span>
              Restating what the client wants does not explain how the
              proposed team will deliver it.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Writing before planning
            </strong>

            <span>
              Starting with prose often produces a polished description
              of an approach the team has never actually agreed upon.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Generic methodology
            </strong>

            <span>
              Standard corporate process should be adapted to the actual
              project rather than pasted into the proposal unchanged.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Isolated disciplines
            </strong>

            <span>
              Separate technical narratives can hide the coordination
              required to deliver one integrated project.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Activities without purpose
            </strong>

            <span>
              A list of things the team will do is weaker than an approach
              that explains what those activities accomplish.
            </span>

          </div>


          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Empty value-added claims
            </strong>

            <span>
              Value-added ideas should solve an identifiable client or
              project problem, not simply promise extra effort.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         SASHA
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-sasha"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-heading">

          <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
            WORKING WITH SASHA
          </p>

          <h2 id="methodology-sasha">
            Give Sasha the team's thinking, not just the RFP.
          </h2>

        </div>


        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Sasha can help organize the RFP scope, identify gaps in the
            developing work plan, and turn a completed storyboard into
            proposal-ready methodology content.
          </p>

          <p>
            But the strongest methodology still depends on the knowledge
            of the people who will deliver the project. Use the Rapid Fire
            session to capture that knowledge first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then use Sasha to help structure, test, refine, and write the
            resulting approach while preserving the team's project-specific
            thinking.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    {{!-- =================================================
         FINAL PRINCIPLE
    ================================================== --}}

    <section
      class="sasha-practice-page-section"
      aria-labelledby="methodology-final"
    >

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
          THE PRINCIPLE
        </p>

        <h2 id="methodology-final">
          Plan the work. Connect the work. Then write the story.
        </h2>

        <p>
          A persuasive methodology demonstrates that the proposed team
          understands not only what the client needs, but how the project
          will actually be delivered. Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding
          creates that delivery logic before proposal writing begins.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


{{!-- =================================================
     DECISION
================================================== --}}

<section
  class="sasha-practice-page-section
         sasha-practice-page-decision"
  aria-labelledby="methodology-decision"
>

  <div class="sasha-practice-decision-panel">

    <div>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-kicker">
        ADD TO THIS PURSUIT?
      </p>

      <h2 id="rapid-fire-decision">
        Would you like to use a Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding exercise?
      </h2>

      <p>
        Sasha will use this choice when creating the proposal
        schedule and will include this practice if you select Yes.
      </p>

    </div>


    <form
      method="POST"
      action="/plan/practice-decision"
      class="sasha-practice-decision-form"
    >

      {{#if csrfToken}}

        <input
          type="hidden"
          name="_csrf"
          value="{{csrfToken}}"
        >

      {{/if}}


      <input
        type="hidden"
        name="pursuitId"
        value="{{proposal._id}}"
      >


      <input
        type="hidden"
        name="practiceKey"
        value="methodology"
      >


      <fieldset class="sasha-practice-page-options">

        <legend class="visually-hidden">
          Use Rapid Fire Methodology Storyboarding
        </legend>


<label>

  <input
    type="radio"
    name="decision"
    value="yes"
    {{#if practiceSelected}}
      checked
    {{/if}}
  >

  <span>
    Yes
  </span>

</label>


<label>

  <input
    type="radio"
    name="decision"
    value="no"
    {{#unless practiceSelected}}
      checked
    {{/unless}}
  >

  <span>
    No
  </span>

</label>

      </fieldset>


      <button
        type="submit"
        class="sasha-plan-card-action"
      >
        Save Choice
      </button>

    </form>

  </div>

</section>

  </div>

</main>